# Trivy findings that are accepted, with the reason.
#
# Format: one check ID per line. Add a comment above every entry explaining why
# it is safe here — an unexplained suppression is indistinguishable from a bug
# someone silenced to make CI green. Review each entry when the related
# resource changes.

# AVD-AWS-0089 — S3 bucket does not have logging enabled.
# Applies to the access log bucket itself. Pointing a log bucket at itself
# creates a feedback loop, where each log write generates another log entry;
# AWS recommends against it. The site bucket and CloudFront both log into it,
# and the state bucket is written only by Terraform, whose own audit trail is
# CloudTrail plus the state file's version history.
AVD-AWS-0089

# AVD-AWS-0132 — S3 bucket does not encrypt data with a customer managed key.
# All three buckets (site, log, Terraform state) use SSE-S3 (AES256), which is
# explicitly sanctioned in CLAUDE.md ("AES256 (or aws:kms in prod)"). A
# customer-managed KMS key costs money per key per month and adds rotation and
# key-policy overhead that buys nothing here: the site bucket holds only public
# static assets served through CloudFront, the log bucket holds access logs
# (and per AWS docs, SSE-KMS is not even supported as a target for S3 server
# access logging — see the comment in s3-logs.tf), and the state bucket's
# sensitivity is already addressed by TLS-only access, versioning, and a
# private-by-default policy. Revisit for prod if compliance ever requires CMKs.
AVD-AWS-0132
